{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: the compliance contract is now pinned in five places with no drift guard**

The same two facts are hand-copied across:

- the five dead permissions: `scripts/check_cws_compliance.py` (`DEAD_PERMISSIONS`), `scripts/ext_package_guard.py` (`dead`), `browser_ext/test/v21_manifest_test.mjs` (`DEAD`), `test/browser_ext/cws_compliance_test.dart` (`deadPermissions`);
- the nine mandatory policy sections: `scripts/check_cws_compliance.py` (`MANDATORY_SECTIONS`) and this file (`mandatorySections`).

Each copy is self-consistent today, but nothing fails when one list is extended and another isn't — e.g. a tenth section added to the Python gate but not the Dart test would silently weaken one of the two REG layers. A mechanical drift check is cheap: pin the counts (`DEAD_PERMISSIONS.length == 5`, `mandatorySections.length == 9`) and, better, have one consumer assert the other's literal source (the way this test already greps `build_browser_ext.sh` for `fetch_vendor_assets.sh`), or generate both from a single JSON in `browser_ext/`.
